Now I got it.

My problem was that I didn't know the BSD-style of partitioning.

Here the few steps:

At first, boot from your cdrom with:

>>> boot dka600 -fi /kernels/generic.gz -fl root=/dev/scd0

Your installation should boot now.

Then you will come to the point where you have to fdisk your  harddisk.
Now delete all "normal" partitions. Then type "b" for BSD-disklabel stuff.
There you have to create a huge partition (over the whole harddisk), I placed it
at the third place. Now you create your root and your swap-partition (first and
second place), overlapping the huge partition (it has something of logical
partitions).
You have to set the partition type, the list is a bit different to the normal
partition type list.
For the huge Partition you have to choose BSD4.2.

When you are ready, you go back, back, back and choose diskdruid to set the
mount point /root for the root partition.

Now you can install quite normal, after installation you reboot from cdrom with
root on the harddisk, my command was:
>>>  boot dka600 -fi /kernels/generic.gz -fl "root=/dev/sda1 ro"

The server should boot from your installed linux now.
When you have a bootprompt you type:
swriteboot /dev/sda /boot/bootlx         , perhaps you have to give some
options, but Linux asks for them.

You should be able to boot now from harddisk with:
>>> boot dka0 -fl "1:0"

That's it...

Jan Gemeiner

