Josef Kolbitsch <skol@sbox.remove.for.spam.tugraz.at> wrote:
> Jan Gemeiner <Jan.Gemeiner@dresdner-bank.com> wrote:
>> There you have to create a huge partition (over the whole harddisk), I placed it
>> at the third place. Now you create your root and your swap-partition (first and
>> second place), overlapping the huge partition (it has something of logical
>> partitions).

>    why should i have to create that huge partition over the whole harddisk?

If you will use that disk only with Linux, ever, then there is no need.
Something else, like DU, may need that partition as otherwise it does
not have a full disk access.  A question of compatibility with others.
Traditionally this is a partition 'c'.

> i never did that, and still all my linux installations worked.

Linux does not care one way or another.

BTW - an installer, i.e. anaconda, will write for you BSD type partition
table _provided_ there was not there already another one and even if you
removed all partitions then an old table is still there.  If some other
partition table is found, regardless of a type, then it is taken
seriously.  This is likely what caught Jan and possibly also Nicola.  If
you want to be sure that nothing like that will happen "wipe" a disk
writing a meg of two of nulls to a raw disk before starting installation
if this disk is not straight from factory (and sometimes even then).

Same thing if you are reusing a disk with an old all-covering partition
'c'.  Anaconda will not find a space outside of partition table and 
you will have to write bootblock yourself passing '-f3' flag to
swriteboot.  Anaconda does not know that this partition does not
have any file system on it and also no really sure way to tell if
an you need this old partition table for something or not.

BTW - 'swriteboot' is in a boot image which is uncompressed and upacked
into a ram disk when you are booting into installation/upgrade.  So a
naive search over CD indeed will not find it.

  Michal


