Bob Kaplow wrote:
> 1) What can/will Compaq do when the IPF V2 that Tru64, VMS, and NSK are
> targeted for are as late as Merced?

Since Compaq will continue to build Alpha motherboards and systems, this is
not really important for existing customers since they can continue using
their existing Alphas and add more CPUs.

Where this starts to matter is acquisition of new VMS customers. I think that
Compaq has already acepted the fact that they won't be getting much new VMS
business for the next 5 years. Besides, from Compaq's point of view, I suspect
that new VMS customers represent such a small amount that Compaq can live
without them without its books seing a difference.

Compaq must have factored the FUD factor of killing alpha before IA64 is ready
and most probably took the decision knowing full well what the impact would be
on VMS sales. They are not stupid.
I am convinced that Compaq is perfectly willing to live without new VMS sales
and an ever more focused VMS market niche. They know which customers they want
to keep and they will probably succeed in keeping them.

> 3) What percentage of Compaq / third party / ISV software will make it to
> IA64? Remember there are still things that never got ported to Alpha.

While this is a good question, in reality, it is impossible to answer this
question, especially so early in the process.

Perhaps a better question would be:
What concrete steps will Compaq take to get third party VMS software ported to
IA64 ?
Which Compaq software products (for VMS) will be ported to IA64 ?
Will any VAX-only software get ported to IA64 ?

> 5) If we do migrate from Alpha to IA64 why should we buy from Compaq instead
> of Dell, Gateway, or some vendor that hasn't burned us repeatedly?

Because VMS will be certified to run on that Compaq gear. Those customers who
remain on VMS want the reliability and support. They don't want Dell to blame
Compaq-VMS, and Compaq-VMS blaming Dell. Besides, if VMS only boots on
specific Compaq gear, the question becomes moot.

I think that the Tandem side is much more obvious:  NSK probably won't boot on
low end IA64 servers, it will boot only on specific machines built with the
redundancy and additional hardware to support NSK.

> 7) When Alpha came out to succeed VAX, the end of life for VAX wasn't
> announced until 5 years after Alpha started shipping.


Alpha was meant as a successor to VAX because VAX was slow CISC while Alpha
was blazing fast RISC. Compaq's move for IA64 is meant as a way to get rid of
a portion of its business Compaq didn't care much for: Alpha.

So we are back in the Palmer era where the company is shedding assets it
doesn't consider "core".


> 8) Wouldn't selling Alpha to Intel have been a better decision if it was
> done back in 1997 along with the FAB6 facility.

Yep. In hindsight it would have been. But it would have taken strong wording
and quick action on Intel's point of view to show that Intel was serious about
making Alpha a mainstream product.


> 9) If Compaq had to sell Alpha, couldn't it have been sold to someone who
> wanted to continue the product line instead of killing it in an
> anti-competitive move?

From the Compaq shareholder point of view, the deal which brought Compaq the
most money would be the one to choose, no matter what the philosophical
implications would have been.

Of course, one could argue that had Compaq sold Alpha "whole" to AMD with AMD
continuing Alpha development, then Compaq would not have had to pay to port
all the software to IA64. Remember that at the Compaq announcement, the
concept of standardizing on a single chip was portrayed as a cost saving
mesaure (even though this is unlikely to happen since systems such as NSK will
still require specialised hardware).

So by going to a single platform, Compaq can try to convince the Wall Street
Casino analysts that it is streamlining production and reducing costs.

Intel was probably willing to pay more to get the bits and pieces of Alpha
(patents, engineers) than AMD was willing to pay to get the Alpha chip as a
whole. 

> 11) Where does this leave API? Will legacy manufacturing of Alpha processors
> continue without Compaq?

Alpha systems will continue to be manufactured just like VAX systems continued
to be manufactured long after the chips stopped being fabbed. API may continue
to exist to serve very specific markets until IA64 truly has better
performance. (I am thinking of the likes of the rendering farms for special
effects for movies where the small perforance edge of an alpha would make a
significant enough difference).

But as soon as IA64 truly gets better performance than Alpha, then API will be
dead for such customers.

> 13) How is this anything but an anti-competitive move by Intel to kill off
> the only competition that would show how bad IA64 really is?

Competition laws usually assume the predator will kill off the weaker ones. It
does not deal well with suicidal moves by weaker ones to prevent a predatory
attack by the big bully.

> 14) How long before Dell or Gateway buys Compaq for it's service business?

Dell already has some sort of agreement with IBM for service/support. I think
that the most likely candidate to buy Compaq would be a company such as Sony
or some other asian electronics outfit.


