But that doesn't reflect poorly on Intel.

I think those of us who are absolutely livid at what Compaq has done need to
try to stay on-topic and factual if we're to have any hope of holding them
accountable for it:  they seem to have plenty of apologists willing to go to
bat for them, both inside and outside the company, and while we need to call
attention to their attempts to distract the discussion from the facts if we
ourselves get distracted they will carry the day.

The core points seem to be

1.  That Compaq felt absolutely no obligation to stick to its repeated,
'unequivocal', 'long-term' (with explicit references through EV10)
commitments to Alpha (thanks for that
http://www.compaq.com/alphaserver/news/commit_letter.html URL).

2.  That Compaq felt absolutely no obligation to try to negotiate its way
out of those commitments rather than break then unilaterally.

3.  That Compaq felt absolutely no shame in trying to present this treachery
in a positive light, without even a hint of apology.

4.  That Compaq in particular lied when it said that its Alpha engineers had
told it that Alpha could not maintain a performance lead.

5.  That Compaq is lying in its statements to the effect that this migration
is what its customers have 'consistently' told it they want.

6.  That Compaq's apologists are lying on its behalf when they contend that
Alpha development was too costly to keep the product profitable.

(Feel free to point out any omissions - it's sometimes hard to keep track of
everything Compaq should be held accountable for.)


The document referred to above makes it clear that, contrary to Fred's
assertion, Compaq *does* reassess some of its decisions.  So the
possibilities from here on forward appear to be

a) That the current management will realize the extent of their screw-up and
reassess this one (which would have to include major compensatory supporting
actions) in time to prevent the immediate exodus of a significant fraction
of their customers plus major additional degradation over time, or

b) That the current management will plow on ahead, lose those customers, and
get replaced (but too late) as a result, or

c) That the current management will be replaced quickly enough by new
management that will help repair and compensate for the damage.


I suppose there also exists some possibility that Compaq will survive this
screw-up with its current management intact, but that seems unlikely.  That
probably leaves (b) above as the most likely option, but if I didn't believe
that (a) and (c) had at least a chance there wouldn't be much point in all
this.

- bill





