Hi,

Gary Huntress <ghuntress@mediaone.net> wrote:

> One friend did a quick test compiling the irc client epic.   On his PPro 180
> with 96 meg of ram he says it took 2 minutes and 1 second.   Timing it on
> this alpha it was over 4 minutes.....

> Any explanations for this dramatically bad comparison?   I wasn't expecting
> it to be quite this bad!

Alphas are usually seemingly worse in compilation compared to ix86, because
they are RISC machines. To translate a source code into RISC language is
much more work for a compiler than to translate it into CISC language. This
is also obvious from the size of the binaries.

Just as an example, the size of the binary of e2fsck (Version 1.18-3, Debian
2.2):

m68k-Linux (CISC 32 Bit)	 68948	
PPC-Linux (RISC 32 Bit)		 81728
Alpha-Linux (RISC 64 Bit)	110168

I don't know whether it is useful to simply compare the sizes, perhaps
there are modifications in the source codes for each platform.
It would have been nice to compare a large binary such as the kernel, but
the hardware components are too different for the three platforms.

I've heard that compiling a kernel with modules and everything on an Itanium
processor takes more than 2 hours although it is supposed to be faster than
a normal Pentium. The situation is similar: The translation into machine
language is more complex.
 


Regards,

Kerstin

