post hours before it has been written is in any need of help from me, but in
case your crystal ball just clouded up:

Alpha is a RISC microcomputer architecture created by DEC about a decade ago
and acquired by Compaq 3 years ago (along with what was left of DEC after
CEO Robert Palmer ran it into the ground).

Alpha established its performance supremacy in the industry shortly after
its introduction in 1992 (IIRC), has held it for all but a few months of the
time since then, and showed every indication of being able to continue to do
so for as long as its owner cared to develop it.  It was fatally wounded a
month ago when Compaq declared its intent to accept 30 pieces of silver from
Intel to stop Alpha development after the currently complete major iteration
of the architecture makes it into production in about 18 months:  bad news
for Alpha customers, but very good news indeed for Intel's new 'Itanium'
microprocessor, which performs embarrassingly poorly considering that Intel
has been shoveling money into its development for close to as long as Alpha
has existed.

So an 'Alpha machine' is very likely a PC or minicomputer based on an Alpha
microprocessor.  If it's old, it may say 'digital' on the front; if newer,
'Compaq' or possibly 'API'.

Since Alpha was developed by DEC, it is used to running operating systems of
high-or-better quality.  VMS (which some might call 'The operating system
currently known as OpenVMS') is preferred, but Tru64 Unix (previously
Digital Unix, born as OSF/1, and with prior ancestry of a decidedly
mongreloid nature) is acceptable and Linux a more recent but happily
boisterous option.

However, as a special concession to industry-standard mediocrity, Alphas can
also run Alpha-specific versions of Windows NT:  the Alpha instruction set
differs rather markedly from the Intel x86 instruction set, and the
operating system itself must thus be a special version of NT, though many
native x86 applications can be run via emulation.  Alpha cannot, however,
run released versions of Windows 2000, since Compaq screwed up that option
about 2 years ago (along with the opportunity to have had exclusive use of
64-bit Win2K on Alpha for a year or more while Intel continued to fumble its
Itanium release).

Thus to load Windows NT onto an Alpha system, you need an Alpha-specific
Windows NT distribution kit.  And, as I said, a suitably apologetic
attitude.  Having a VMS (or Tru64, or Linux) distribution kit ready to
reinstall after you're finished doing whatever you needed to do with NT
might well improve your Alpha's willingness to tolerate the temporary
indignity.

- bill





