Helen:

Alpha refers to the DEC Computer Company (now Compaq) 64 bit processor.
In layman's terms, your PeeCee has a 32 bit processor, the Alpha has a 64
bit processor.  It's capacity for floating point computing (read
Arithmetic here) is many times more than the top end Wintel PeeCee box
available today.  There are documented cases of Alpha Servers being
online continuously for many years.  That is not to say that these
machines are running buggy OS's like NT, they are more likely using Unix
or OpenVMS.  These OS's are far more stable and fault tolerant than any
of Bill's products.

Installing NT onto an Alpha is a fairly straightforward, I'll just gloss
over the specific parts because different Alpha's will be slightly
different.
First figure out if you are using ARC or SRM Console. You can do it with
either, but ARC is easiest for beginner. Simply turn on the system, if it
shows a pretty Blue Screen that has Menus it's in ARC, if it stops at a
black screen with a prompt that looks like this:
>>>
Then it's in SRM.
Depending on which Alpha you have, the command to switch from SRM to ARC
can be as simple as ARC <cr>, then power cycle the system.  Read the
Manual.
Put NT disk into CD Rom.
Select appropriate menu option to install software. The file you'll need
is on the CD Rom in the Alpha sub directory called ARCINST.COM. The
command will look something like this:
cdrom:\alpha\arcinst.com
From there you are on your own.  You will need to know how to configure
NT for your own specific needs.

FWIW: Reconsider your choice of OS's.  Putting NT on an Alpha is a little
like putting a Volkswagen engine in a Ferrari.

Tom C.
Alpha Fan, got 8 of them.




