Good for you.

> I had the following problem: I was able to make a boot floppy, but when
> booting the system don't know where to look for the aboot.conf. I copied
> that file to the diskette but nothing happened.
>
> I tried the possibility with swriteboot. I reserved 2 Megs for aboot at
> the beginning of my harddisk. Doesn't it matter that the partition table
> is erased when installing aboot? I didn't want to try because I thought
> I'd have to reinstall and when partitioning, my bootsektor would be
> rewritten and aboot erased.
>
> Can anybody give me a little hint??

RH generally doesn't support ABoot (needed to boot from SRM console), but
instead, supports MILO (needed to boot from ARC console). I mean, yes, they
can boot from SRM, using ABoot, but they will not install ABoot, no matter
what you do.

The only way to preserve your nerves, is to get Compaq's JumpStart CD. You
can download CD ISO image, ready for burning, from their site:

http://opensource.compaq.com/sourceforge/project/?group_id=3

With it you can do all preparations, as well as post-festum bootstrap loader
installation. The way to go is: Make all partitions "keep off the grass" -
outside first two cylinders on the disk, except for "c". And choose to
install aboot loader. Sure, it will install with its defaults (maybe that
can be changed), but you get an aboot loader and can boot the system, then
edit /etc/aboot.conf and update your aboot.

Nix.




