But the GS320 uses a "cluster in a can" approach to running the database
vs Sun/IBM/HP which use a single instance of the database to get their
numbers.  No one with a sane mind would run 8 instances of Oracle on a
single box.  The only way the GS320 is able to get good numbers is by 
partitioning the box into 8 separate pieces to minimize the cross cell 
latency.  Sun does not have to do this with its UE10K, IBM doesn't do this 
with the p680 and HP does not do this with Superdome.  Only the GS320 needs 
take advantage of the easily partitioned TPC-C test to obtain good
single box numbers.

--Chris
