Compaq on June 25 announced that it will terminate all Alpha development
beyond EV7n and consolidate its entire 64-bit platform and OS lineup on the
IPF architecture beginning in 2004. New Alpha systems and upgrades will be
rolled out through 2004, and Compaq will immediately begin porting its NSK,
Tru64 UNIX, and VMS OSes to the IPF while transferring key Alpha processor
and compiler technology, tools, and engineering resources to Intel.

That's what we know so far. Compaq has yet to provide an explanation of how
"the essence of Alpha" will live on in the IPF architecture. Plenty of
questions about the architectural transition and consolidation remain
unanswered, and there are varying opinions about the impact of this
decision. In an effort to assess the mood of the Alpha customer base and to
gain an understanding of how the user community perceives the Alpha to IPF
architectural transition, we've posted a twenty-question survey on the
Tru64.org Web site at http://www.tru64.org.

The survey shouldn't take more than a couple of minutes to complete. It will
be available from July 14th to July 27th. Email addresses are for
verification only and will not be used for any other purpose. Answering all
questions is required. If A summary of results will be posted in Shannon
knows Compaq and on Tru64.org in the near future.


Ken Farmer, kfarmer@tru64.org
Tru64.org, http://www.tru64.org

Terry C. Shannon
Shannon Knows Compaq
