> how do the DBM-type api's fit into this picture?

I used the "imbedded SQL" preprocessors with Oracle and Informix.  The
preprocessors C or Fortran (or COBOL or whatever) source with imbedded
SQL-ish statements into calls to the DBM's runtime library.  It's a cute
idea.

Back in the mid 1980s, I used Unify on Venix (Unix System III for IBM PC
hardware), and I don't remember the API being all that complex.  But
then, I was temporary, and wrote my database-using applications by
copy-and-paste....
 
> are they typically part of the C run-time ?

Not the ones I used.

> are the DBM api's portable bwtween Unices?

The embedded SQL interfaces are pretty portable.  In fact, both Informix
and Oracle had (nearly?) identical state-reporting or error-reporting
structures, with the same unused fields; I've always assumed they were
borrowed from some IBM SQL interface, maybe the one from DB2.

> Could one move a DBM mapped file
> (backing store file, *.dir?) btween unices?

I would expect them to be significantly portable in many cases, but I
never tried.  As somebody else said, it was unsupported, and there are
endian-ness (and other) issues.

--Kevin Strietzel
