> Veritas has come out with new versions of its Volume Manager and File System
> which it calls Sanpoint Foundation Suite HA. These are versions of VM and FS
> that allow for simultaneous shared access of storage resources. This is the
> basic ingredient in building load-balanced applications.

That may be true in the Intel world.

However, anyone who's ever seen a VAX or an Alpha knows what a console
is. If the devices are not visible at the console, then the o.s. must
rely on higher-level services (such as the one you mentioned).

The truly "basic ingredient" is the ability of the machine to "see" the
shared storage devices, even when there's no o.s. running (yet). The
application doesn't need to care whether or not it's running on a
stand-alone machine or a node in a cluster. The o.s. should provide the
necessary facilties (such OpenVMS's distributed lock management,
intra-cluster communications (SCS) and others) to make this a virtual
non-issue.

Given recent announcements, expect IA64 BIOSes to have a level of
intelligence similar to that which we've seen in VAXes (and later
Alphas) since the early 1990's. When(If) OpenVMS on IA64 finally
arrives, expect to see a quantum leap in cluster flexibility and
functionality.

Remember: Digital invented "clustering" for VAX/VMS - we've had it since
the middle to late 80's and on into OpenVMS for VAX and Alpha. Everyone
else is now playing "catch up".

