> > Tru64, for two) *do* have file systems that
> >
> > a) allow concurrent access to the same data from multiple nodes and
>
> Depending on your exact definition of "concurrent"...

Perhaps.  Using an exacting definition, *no* existing system provides
concurrent access to data at its persistent location (or that location's
cache):  accesses are always serialized in some manner.

>
> NFS allows concurrent access to the same data from multiple nodes, but I
> wouldn't call a collection of NFS clients/servers a cluster (but my
> definition of cluster is rather VMS-biased. 8-).

That's because you're looking at implementation rather than external
function.

Do you consider a VMS cluster that uses only node-private storage (exported
for cluster-wide use) not to be a cluster for that reason?  Such a cluster
uses node-to-node communication to access data managed by another node,
but - especially when combined with distributed volume-shadowing - it sure
works just about the same way as a cluster based on HSxs.

Or do you really feel that the distinction between node-to-node access at
the file level vs. node-to-node access at the device level is important?
The only difference I can see is that the latter potentially allows greater
scalability - but is there some lower limit (greater than 1) on how many
nodes it takes to make something a 'cluster'?

>
> Last time I worked with Tru64 (V5.1 I think), the Cluster File System
(CFS)
> worked by having one of the nodes in the cluster serve the underlying
AdvFS
> file system to the other nodes over the cluster memory channel. Therefore,
> only that node could directly access the underlying file system, and it
only
> looked like the other nodes had direct access,

So what?  See above discussion.

 with CFS server failover
> happening as necessary. This is somewhat different to VMS, where nodes in
a
> cluster can access the file system concurrently and independently.

Different in implementation, certainly.  Different in user-perceived
function?  Hardly at all.  VMS does have faster recovery from node-failure
than current fail-over mechanisms provide, but there's no *intrinsic* reason
that fail-over - especially planned fail-over - need take more than a second
or two.

And as I said above, shared-disk clusters (and even pseudo-shared-disk
clusters using exported node-private drives) can potentially scale
homogeneously to larger sizes than data-partitioned Unix clusters can.
OTOH, where data *is* readily partitionable, partitioned approaches can
scale far larger than the VMS approach can (you and even I may think that
using 'mount points' to cobble many base filesystems together into one large
one feels kind of kludgey, but Unix people seem to like it a lot, and they
do have some credible reasons for this).  So that's something of a wash.

In closing, I haven't yet found anything in your response that calls any
statement (or implication) I made into question.  If you feel I missed
something, please elucidate.

