Yeah, given that Unix is a half-dozen years older than VMS, one could
reasonably observe that it's been a dramatically slow learner in many areas.
Even in more recent conference papers, as often as not one sees no
references to IBM and DEC products (and perhaps others that I just don't
happen to be familiar with) that have been on the market for years offering
much in the way of the novel approaches being discussed.  I suspect that
most academics are as little-steeped in real-world engineering as most
engineers are in academic work, and Unix advances have seemed mostly to come
from academia.

>
> Sigh, and most Un*x and MS admins still have never heard of a ISAM file
structure
> and are happily paying millions to Oracle, MS, etc., etc. because no Un*x
vendor
> would **DARE** to draw the ire of one of their major vendors by including
ISAM
> support "native".

I still hope to get my act together and create a light-weight
record-structured standard file system that would open their eyes.  But
ISAM/VSAM from IBM and RMS from DEC did a pretty good job of scaring people
away (and I say this as one of the original RMS developers):  the interfaces
were unbelievably convoluted, and though some of the higher-level languages
softened them a bit they still tended to make most of the options available
if you really wanted them and hence weren't really all that much simpler in
the end.

For most of the last decade processor cycles and memory have been cheap
enough to avoid the necessity for a great many of the options that made RMS
so complex to approach at the FAB/RAB/NAM/XAB level:  it could be so much
simpler now.  There aren't too many people any more interested in making a
career out of understanding the intricacies of some vendor's file system,
and it would be nice to be able to offer the rest of the world a third
option to either using Oracle (and the human DB administrator that comes
along with such use) or kludging up something on their own.

