"Jack Patteeuw" <jjpatteeuw@peoplepc.com> wrote in message
news:3B43BF3C.705CD606@peoplepc.com...

...

> I can not imagine why any owner of a "group" of NFS mounted systems would
> want to allow **SILENT** multiple write accesses to the same file !!!  It
> sends shudders down my spine knowing that what "Joe" just wrote, "Suzie"
can
> overwrite in a blink, and with out file version numbering, there is no way
> that the original data can be save/recovered.

I must be missing something here:  VMS certainly allows concurrent write
(and over-write) access to files as well.  Versioning only comes into play
when an application deliberately creates a new version of a file and then
(typically, as with an editor) copies into that new version a modified
version of the old version - and while it's likely that the old version will
stick around for a while, it's by no means guaranteed (since old-version
clean-up is a per-directory-settable parameter).

I liked versioning, but it never caught on with the rest of the world, and
the rest of the world does manage to survive without it.

>
> So why does CDFS (maybe with RRE) have file version numbering (on a file
> system that is typically written once) and no other Un*x file system does
?

Probably because Andy Goldstein was DEC's representative to the ISO
committee that designed the CDFS standard.  But it could also have something
to do with the write-once nature of the medium (CD-R technology was on the
horizon back then, but not necessarily CD-RW):  versioning is a way to allow
*effective* over-writes of existing files just by creating a new version -
and since the old version is going to stick around anyway, having the syntax
to access it is nice.

>
> There are pluses and minuses in most modern operating systems (except
those
> from MS, which are just made in the image and likeness of their god), so
why
> aren't these being taught in most colleges and why hasn't the world of
"open
> source" borrowed a few other "good ideas" ?

Versioning just wasn't a good *enough* idea to overcome the fact that no one
else used it.  RMS wasn't a simple enough good idea (or alternatively some
would say that its complexity moved it completely out of the 'good idea'
column).

The Unix approach to handling files as byte-streams, while a bit primitive
for some use, was, and remains, a good idea.  The first implementation was
unbelievably inefficient, and even today a lot aren't that great, but the
*idea* was good (though I'd still like to see it supplemented by a
cleanly-designed record-handling facility).

- bill

