Interesting in the following sense: 
	single filesystem served from single fileserver machine with a
	logical volume manager mounting network block devices from other
	machines with lots of disks

is I believe do-able today with stock linux and NFS.

	single filesystem served from multiple machines with lots of
	disks without fileserver+LVM+NBD 

would be well worth reading the papers on how it worked. 

It might be that something approaching the latter could be created from
a collection of machines serving disks to a collection of fileservers
that collaborated to ensure their clients *saw* a coherent filesystem
without having to create a coherent filesystem. 

So, rather than storage boxes ---> fileserver ---> clients
				   with replication

we have
	storage boxes ---> fileserver ---> clients
				^
				|
				V	
	storage boxes ---> fileserver ---> clients
				^
				|
				V
	storage boxes ---> fileserver ---> clients

with redundancy built in such that, after a write is committed, loss of
any one piece of hardware is not a problem.

I guess this requires the fileserver<->fileserver bandwidth to
approximate the aggregate write-bandwidth of the clients, whilst
permitting the aggregate read-bandwith of the clients to saturate all
available links.

But the case I'm interested in does not differentiate between
fileservers and clients. All boxes in the group provide storage to the
common storage pool.


