"Peter Mayne" <Peter.Mayne@au1.ibm.com> wrote in
<9i02qf$rik$1@news.btv.ibm.com>: 
>Last time I worked with Tru64 (V5.1 I think), the Cluster File System
>(CFS) worked by having one of the nodes in the cluster serve the
>underlying AdvFS file system to the other nodes over the cluster memory
>channel. Therefore, only that node could directly access the underlying
>file system, and it only looked like the other nodes had direct access,
>with CFS server failover happening as necessary. This is somewhat
>different to VMS, where nodes in a cluster can access the file system
>concurrently and independently. 
>

Nope - that's not how it works, at least in a connected cluster.

Though a single node serves the metadata to other nodes via
the cluster interconnect, each node that has a direct path to the 
given disk uses that direct path (or multiple paths if they are 
available) to access any actual file data. 

They do do this concurrently - multiple nodes initiate io's at 
the same time.  Of course, with single disks, it wouldn't *really*
be concurrent, but large raid sets surely would.

If a cluster is not fully connected to all the storage, or if a path
failure occurs from one node, then the cluster interconnect is still
used for data access (i.e. it would be "served" data.)

This architecture is actually quite scalable, and failure resiliant, 
especially since all nodes can be serving file system metadata for
different filesystems.  The meta data traffic is still substantially
less than the lock traffic that would be required to do fine-grain
lock access, and it also permits more rapid recovery if a node 
fails.




