"Rich Walker" <rw@shadow.org.uk> wrote in message
news:m33d8cvsy7.fsf@lin-pc.shadow.local...
> "Bill Todd" <billtodd@foo.mv.com> writes:

...

> > The above diagram - with all the 'fileserver' entries removed - is what
was
> > being recently discussed in comp.arch/comp.arch.storage in the
'commodity
> > storage servers' thread (the storage boxes cooperate to serve files or
space
> > on logical volumes directly to clients).
>
> AFAICT that discussion was more about what would go *in* the storage
> boxes. The issue of how to coherently generate a filesystem across a
> collection of collaborating boxes seemed to get glossed over, though
> could have missed it.

The discussion originated with a question on my part about whether anyone
knew of a reason why approaches rooted in Stonebraker & Schloss' early work
on expanding RAID concepts to distributed servers (such as the Berkeley
"Serverless Network File System" xFS work and Ed Lee's 'Petal' project for
DEC) had never resulted in commodity commercial products.  I never got any
kind of an answer, I assume because either  a) the topic is a bit too
specialized for most readers of those newsgroups or  b) the readers for whom
it's not too specialized aren't talking.

So the topic wasn't intended to be an architecture discussion, and the
architecture includes both some things that I'm not free to discuss and
other things that I'm not sure I want to discuss at the moment (having an
interest in product development in this area).

>
> I can see how you might generate a load-balancing logical volume with
> an intra-storage-box broadcast protocol for attach and detach, which
> raises an interesting question of what amount of redundancy is necessary
> in such a system given reasonable rates of removal of components...

Increased redundancy costs write performance (though you may get a bit of it
back on reads).  It can also cost complexity if it attempts to handle
frequent failures.  So I'm inclined to think, especially given that
component costs are low and continuing to decrease, that the minimal amount
of redundancy consistent with the desired availability under the assumption
that outages are only failure-related rather than simply the result of a
workstation reboot makes sense.

...

> > But the clients have to trust each
> > other a lot more than when they're separated from the storage servers
and
> > can rely upon storage-server protection mechanisms.  Clients that
cooperate
> > to export a single, stable application to a much wider external world
(e.g.,
> > Web servers) tend to fall into this category, but clients that may be
> > running virtually anything (which can compromise their robustness) or
are in
> > the service of individuals (e.g., workstations) who may elect to reboot
them
> > at any time may better be kept separate from the storage servers.
>
> That's the redundancy problem. I assume that someone, somewhere, has
> done the equivalent of merging the spare disk space on a network into
> one big storage pool, and has dealt with the "random reboot" problem by
> a RAID-type technique.
>
> I've been considering it from the low-end, where each machine has disk
> space of a few tens of GB, and reboot probabilities are fairly low, but
> machines might be very slow from time to time. However, without a
> implementation of a filesystem that supports multiple machines each
> generating the same logical volume as each other from the available
> data, I can't see how to do it.

That sounds something like the Berkeley 'Network of Workstations' ('NOW')
effort, of which the work I mentioned above was a part.  But as I said,
components are cheap enough that using anything other that relatively stable
nodes to serve the storage (such that additional redundancy would be
required to make up for the vastly decreased reliability) may not make
sense:  it doesn't take a very large average percentage of nodes being
off-line to start consuming inordinate resources as they recover and must be
brought back up to date (well, I suppose that could be considered a research
area, but I'm not sure that the problem justifies it given that it would
almost certainly increase the complexity of an already-complex system and
that an inexpensive hardware solution exists).

>
> So, my next question is: is there a filesystem that allows each machine in
> the network to generate the same image as all the others, and maintain
> synchronisation between them, and cope with permanent loss of parts of
> the storage?

Not that I know of.  VMS's ODS-2 file system might, if it had much more
comprehensive underlying volume management (effectively virtualizing the
distributed storage in a more-than-RAID-like manner).  IBM's GPFS on AIX may
be somewhat similar to VMS in this regard (and VSAM on Parallel Sysplex has
some similar capabilities, but is otherwise a bit weirder than many people
wish to deal with, at least as a common file system).  Most other
distributed file systems either partition the data (like the Unix systems
previously mentioned in this thread) or have a central meta-data server that
tells the clients how to access shared storage devices directly and performs
centralized synchronization (IBM's - nee Mercury Computer's - SANergy,
HP's - nee Transoft Networks' - <forget the name>, Adic's - nee
Mountaingate's - <another name I forget>, the new extensions to EMC's Celera
server, Avid's Unity/Trilligent file server product).

The (new) Tru64 file system is a bit of a hybrid, since it combines
mount-point-style aggregation of single-node-served (fail-overable)
filesystems with a distributed caching/locking mechanism.  And I don't know
what the Tricord Lunar Flare stuff looks like inside, but AFAIK it's a
storage-level rather than a file-level server.

There are a whole raft of file systems under development on Linux.  The main
one that supports shared access to storage devices is GFS, which has some
underlying similarities to the VMS clustering approach but got off on the
wrong foot by being based on device-level lock support (Seagate may have see
n this as a potential market differentiator, but unfortunately it just isn't
a good idea) and AFAIK has never really recovered (though it now has
modularized its distributed lock management and provides an alternate flavor
that requires no special device firmware support).  Several other ambitious
Linux cluster file system efforts have started and then appeared to languish
over the past couple of years - at least a couple having gotten diverted
into more basic cluster infrastructure work (since a cluster file system
requires things like heartbeat and membership facilities that are central to
other aspects of cluster functioning as well).

So while all the technology necessary to build the kind of distributed file
system you describe certainly exists, AFAIK no one has put it together into
anything like the product you're interested in (though the Berkeley work may
come fairly close).

