Peter Mayne wrote:

> 
> Last time I worked with Tru64 (V5.1 I think), the Cluster File System (CFS)
> worked by having one of the nodes in the cluster serve the underlying AdvFS
> file system to the other nodes over the cluster memory channel. Therefore,
> only that node could directly access the underlying file system, and it only
> looked like the other nodes had direct access, with CFS server failover
> happening as necessary. This is somewhat different to VMS, where nodes in a
> cluster can access the file system concurrently and independently.

Indeed, and this can be a performance problem. We have seen instances on
our cluster where some I/O bottlenecks are apparent when the I/O is to a
file system served from another node which do not appear if the I/O is
to local devices.

Having also managed VMS clusters with disks on shared busses where if a
host can see the disk it can access the file system this seems a
drawback of Trucluster compared to VMS clusters.

In the defence of Trucluster I would quickly add that Trucluster is
getting there as more functionality is rolled out. While it would be
great for Trucluster to have the same functionality that has been built
up over the years in VMS clusters on day one this is probably a little
