From my limited experience, most manufacturers of so-called
minicomputers then engaging in UNIX usually had ISAM available at least
as layered product, in order to provide their existing COBOL or just
typical commercial applications on the new OS. Relational databases at
that time just began to enter the scene, because they were probably
munching away far too many cycles from the main applications to still
provide a decent response time to the dozens of terminals.

Even in '94 I was told that some small vendor just used ISAM, because
the SQL stuff was just too big and slow. 

And nowadays... What do you expect? Students must know about relational
DBs, and their professors like to tell them about nifty extensions for
multimedia or even OODBs, so there is of course no/few time to teach
them about such obsolete stuff like ISAM !?

