LBohan@dbc.spam_less..com wrote:
> 

> for those of you with greater experience w/
> more varied versions of UNIX  than myself,
> 
Not that I'm an expert, but I just found the old XPG3 book on Data
Management.


> how do the DBM-type api's fit into this picture?
> 

Compared with C-ISAM they are very poor. C-ISAM has about 20 functions
in its API, locking, erasing of records, multi-part keys, even secondary
index files for a single ISAM file. 

So, one could think of libdbm as a 'poor university's implementation' of
ISAM...

> are they typically part of the C run-time ?

libdbm.a is a BSDism, so I'd guess that early SystemV R2 and 3 did not
come with it - unless they were 'value-added'... Even then, the hash
file is very 'holey' (large empty gaps of NULs), so that large indices
consume a lot of space if the filesystem does not support sparse files.
E.g., backing up the index files of old SunOS/Solaris AnswerBook
installations was no joy.

> 
> are the DBM api's portable bwtween Unices?

Fairly the same. 
> 
> Could one move a DBM mapped file
> (backing store file, *.dir?) btween unices?

Mmm. Officially no. If the word sizes and endianess is the same, perhaps
yes. This also depends on the alignment rules of the compiler.


