In article <9avaktsl34dldif5623ac3krpd1b1uagtv@4ax.com>, Alan Greig <a.greig@virgin.net> writes:
> On Thu, 05 Jul 2001 13:06:13 -0500, Greg Pfister <pfister@us.ibm.com>
> wrote:
> 
>>I definitely credit DEC with coining the term "cluster." The
>>first public use of the term I've found was in: Nancy P.
>>Kronenberg, Henry M. Levy, and William D. Strecker. "Vaxclusters:
>>A Closely-Coupled Distributed System." ACM Transactions on
>>Computer Systems, 4(3):130�146, May 1986.
> 
> I'm fairly certain that DEC used the term "cluster" in an NDA
> Jupiter/TOPS-20 presentation to (the then) Dundee College of
> Technology several years prior to 1986. The cluster concept was later
> ported into VMS. HSC controllers and star couplers were supported on
> TOPS-20 before VMS.

Certainly before shipment of the CI and HSC, the hardware folks thought
a cluster was defined by hardware.  They could not forsee the Local Area
VAXcluster, and maybe the software folks couldn't either.  While those
hardware folks were working on both 36-bit and 32-bit CI/HSC support,
the software folk were doing their own cluster effort to use that
hardware.  Without the software you have (in the 32-bit variant) what
shipped with VMS 3.7, which nobody today would call a cluster.  It was
a solution in search of a problem.  The VMS software made it useful.
I have no idea whether software ever shipped in the 36-bit world.


