Right, SRM is a mini os, we use it to run diags, down load firmware, etc..
We even have system level exercisers that run all the options (memory disk,
tape concurrently under SRM  But.. when you boot (VMS-Unix), it sits in
lower memory
 A ctrl-P in VMS use to bring you back to the console, and a cont, would let
VMS to continue from where it was suspended.

But with Linux, SRM is copied to upper memory where it is stored until
shutdown/halt/reboot,  during which time it is copied back to lower memory
before control is xfered back to the console..  This bizarre behavior is a
result of Linux being run first on a arc/alpha-bios console systems, SRM
support coming later..  this is all in the kernel sources if you want to dig
through it..

as I remember only VMS was capable of switching between the SRM and the os,
but it was designed that way, using callbacks.

JJ
