> From: Paul DeMone <pdemone@igs.net>

> > What killed Alpha? Marketing malfeasance and Stupid Strategy Tricks. Pure
> > and simple.
> 
>   Indeed, what kind of oafs can't market the fastest 64 bit MPU
>   after 8 years in the market?

That's a soothing thought which I am sure is popular among
comp.arch regulars, but in fact there was more to Alpha
downfall than poor marketing.

Alpha systems that I saw (in the 1994-96 frame) had extremely
fragile performance (move printf one sentence, the whole
program works 3 times slower - and the printf was not
in a loop of any kind, just a banner); quite unreliable
(Trials at Demos ended with all their units down with bad
power supplies and Sun won in that tender); there was a
lot of small annoyances too that seem too small to remeber
(proliferation of firmware bases, all broken, just for one
thing - of course such trifles would not bother titans
like Paul).

When I tried to hint that perhaps Alpha _boxes_ were not as great
as Alpha _CPU_ SPECint89 scores suggested, I was laughed out from
comp.arch. Similar thing happened to that guy from Sun UK who
tried to articulate concerns - Andrew Harrison, I think was his
name - everyone just filed him into "Sun lapdog" drawer.

Alpha was dead and irrelevant long before Intel started to
shrink the performace gap. And sure it was not the marketing.
Inability to build reasonably priced, reliable systems sounds
more like it.

-- Pete


