> Certainly the latter is not clustering, just a form of hot-standby.
> Even Microsoft claims to be able to do the latter.

Well, it's the form of clustering that is prevalent in HP-UX and Solaris.
Though in both cases Veritas is beginning to build in some form of
simultaneous operations capability, where you will have the ability to share
disks simultaneously between multiple nodes in a cluster. The inability to
share disks simultaneously has been the big impediment to total clustering
so far. If you can maintain file system integrity without having to recode
applications, then you have made it one step closer to load-sharing
clusters.

    Yousuf Khan




