> You guys really need to read Greg's book (well, Rob needs to re-read it).
> Clustering means many different things to many people, and no one group owns
> the definition.

True, and thanks for the plug, Bill.

More specifically -- even I would be hard-pressed to describe as
"clustering" the kind of thing described in a later thread
branch: roll a machine out of a closet, turn it on, try to get it
working. Lots of luck making that work.

Failover clustering really tries to be a bit better than that --
at least having the "other" machine up and running, with failover
initiated automatically. *That,* in many (not all) contexts is
decidedly nontrivial, usually takes weeks to set up, requires
continuous maintenance, and has to be periodically tested if you
want any confidence at all that it will work.

Some reasons:

First of all, how do you know the other guy failed? Heartbeat.
Um, did he stop, or is he busy right now? (It's a real-time
signal, and few OSs are set up to deal with it right.) Now, are
you *really* sure he's not accessing those disks any more? If you
both start writing them, scrambled data. And so on.

And by the way, are you really sure that application is going to
run after restart on the other system? Did you faithfully
replicate all the tuning, option-setting, updating, etc., you did
on one box to the other? For the app, the middleware, and the OS?
Do you even *know* exactly what you did to get it to work on the
first system?

You haven't used the other paths to those disks recently. Are you
sure the cleaning people didn't jostle a connector loose? And
what about the re-cabling everybody did last week, was it a mite
too tight in some areas? Near those paths? Hmmm?

And, of course, most people aren't satisfied just to leave
another system sitting there doing nothing, just in case. There's
always something else running on it. Are the priorities set right
to keep your customers satisfied? Of course, this isn't "just"
failover clustering any more.

And yes, I would agree with the statement that there is an
advantage Tru64Cluster may have acquired over many others. They
added the single-system image facilities that have a long history
but eventually landed there. This capability makes it look like
there's a single copy of the OS, even though there are multiple
boxes. That helps a lot with some of the problems, particularly
keeping the OS parameters and options and tuning the same on all
the systems. Somewhat helps the same problem for middleware and
apps, but they need rewriting to really eliminate that part of
the problem. It provides advantages, but in many cases they
aren't overwhelming; they could be overshadowed if, for example,
the customer really wanted some form of fancy cascading failover
that another vendor had and Tru didn't.

Is this enough of a difference to win the usual cluster
comparison contest? -- meaning: add up incommeasurate qualities,
see who gets the biggest score -- quite possibly. I think it did
in DH Brown's latest compaison.

By the way, Compaq recently took that capability and applied it
to Linux, open-sourcing the whole thing. Makes you think.
True64Cluster dies with Alpha, right? Just like VMS. Locus lives
on, now in open source.

(By the way, to complete the book plug: "In Search of Clusters,
second edition" published by Prentice Hall. ISBN 0-13-899709-8.
Generally avaiable in bookstores. A couple of URLs:
http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ISBN=0138997098/4308-1385679-076577
(Amazon)
http://www.prenhall.com/ptrbooks/ptr_0138997098.html (Prentice
Hall))

(Sorry, no third edition yet.)

Greg Pfister


