Bill,
    Interesting information.  Thanks for posting it.  As I read the whole
thing, it really "stinks" of old time Digital Employees never admitting the
failure of their original or current company to properly market and advance
the Alpha processor.  The writing has been on the wall for a long time.  Not
many will contest the fact that Alpha was/is an execellent architecture.
But what many folks fail to see is that it is a failure, none-the-less, in
the world of market acceptance.  Another good example is what has happened
with DEC RDB.  When Larry Ellison's little Oracle company bought it from
Digital, all of the Digital employees saw this as a great windfall for RDB.
Eventually Oracle's offering would be merged into the RDB codebase and RDB
would rule-the-day on all pplatforms.  Well, RDB never made it off of VMS,
and the other Oracle product has received a ton of engineering, while RDB
has slowly been left to its own recourse.  I'm not saying that Oracle 8/9
haven't benefited from RDB technology, but the existing RDB users haven't
seen a huge benefit, and they are still locked into VMS.  RDB may have been
better, but it didn't "win" the market over.
    I see the Alpha technology following a similar pattern.  Intel will take
some of the IP they have bought, and will utilize it now that they legally
can.  We will not see Alpha become the baseline for an x86 compatible
processor, IMHO.  To believe so, I feel is short-sighted and egotistical.
I'd love to be wrong about this, but let's be realistic.  The only piece of
Digital still performing well for Compaq is the Storageworks line of
equipment, and the Service and Support organization.  Everything is going by
the wayside.
    The day Digital was bought by a PC manufacturer, foretold the death of a
bygone era.  Intel and Microsoft had won.  When Microsoft dropped Windows NT
support for Alpha, MIPS, and PowerPC, it was also a foreshadowing of the
future.  When the world's most popular OS no longer runs on your CPU, odds
are good that it isn't long for this world.  This is not to say that these
CPU's don't have other uses.  Either in the ultra high-end, or for embedded
applications.  The only real other platforms that are doing OK are SUN's and
IBM's.  Both of them realize that they need to stay up at the enterprise
level as their offerings will never gain wide market acceptance on the
desktop or small server world.

Hey, these are just my opinions, and we all know everyone's got one.  Feel
free to comment via the newsgroup.  I'm not trying to start a holy war, I'm
just a pretty pragmatic person when it comes to using the right tool for the
job.  When 99% of today's computing needs are satisfied by an Intel
platform, it might be wise use Intel platforms for 99% of our applications.

DanO


